+### Data extraction & assertion
+
+  âš  PRE-ASSERTION MODAL CHECK (do this BEFORE every assertion_check call):
+    Take a browser_snapshot. If the snapshot contains a line starting with
+    "- dialog" or "- alertdialog" AND that dialog is NOT itself what you are
+    asserting (i.e. the test is not a "dialog appeared" test), then:
+      1. Dismiss it first: click its close/reject button OR press Escape.
+      2. Wait for it to disappear (check next snapshot no longer shows it).
+      3. THEN take a fresh browser_snapshot and call assertion_check.
+    Rationale: an open dialog overlays the main content. assertion_check on the
+    snapshot-with-dialog will read dialog text instead of page content, producing
+    false passes (dialog happens to contain the expected text) or false failures
+    (main content is unreachable behind the overlay).
+
+  Visible text on the page:
+    â†’ fresh browser_snapshot, then assertion_check with expectedText.
+
+  Computed values, formatted numbers, percentages, prices, or text living
+  in shadow DOM / canvas / virtualised lists (anything that doesn't surface
+  cleanly in the snapshot):
+    â†’ browser_evaluate returning the value as a string or structured object,
+      THEN assertion_check. The platform caches the evaluate result and
+      uses it as a fallback haystack â€” see "Verifying DYNAMIC content".
+
+  Region-specific data (search results, product grids, cart items, data tables,
+  form output, validation messages, order details):
+    âš  WHOLE-PAGE TEXT CHECK IS WRONG HERE. The a11y snapshot contains EVERY word
+    on the page â€” sidebar filters, nav bars, promotional banners, footers ALL
+    contribute. If you call assertion_check(expectedText: "Rs.") and the sidebar
+    shows "Rs. 499" in a promotion, the assertion will PASS even if the product
+    grid is completely empty. That is a false pass.
+
+    â†’ Use browser_evaluate with a SCOPED querySelector to extract text ONLY from
+      the relevant container, THEN assertion_check on that result:
+
+      Example â€” verify search results contain a price:
+        browser_evaluate:
+          document.querySelector('[data-testid="search-results"], .product-grid, .results-list, [role="main"] ul, .items-container')?.textContent || ''
+        Then: assertion_check({ expectedText: "Rs.", containerSelector: ".features_items" })
+
+      Example â€” verify 0 results shown in the results area (not the whole page):
+        browser_evaluate:
+          document.querySelector('.search-results, [role="main"] .no-results, .product-list')?.textContent || ''
+        Then: assertion_check({ expectedText: "No products found", containerSelector: ".search-results" })
+
+    IMPORTANT: always pass containerSelector matching the querySelector you used. This records the assertion
+    scope so the exported test spec asserts against the same container â€” not the whole page â€” preventing
+    false passes from nav/header/footer text matching.
+
+    The optional chaining (?.) is REQUIRED: if the selector matches nothing, the
+    expression returns '' and assertion_check correctly scores not_matched.
+
+    Regions that commonly poison whole-page TEXT checks:
+      â€¢ Left sidebars with filter panels (prices, categories, ratings)
+      â€¢ Promotional/hero banners (prices, item names, offers)
+      â€¢ "You may also like" / "Recently viewed" recommendation widgets
+      â€¢ Footer with payment logos, currency icons
+      â€¢ Breadcrumb navigation (repeats category/page names)
+      â€¢ Tab headers with unread count badges
